<co-content>
 <p variant="body1">
  Bootstrap is often described as a way to "build fast, responsive sites" and as a "feature-packed, powerful, and extensible front-end toolkit."
 </p>
 <p variant="body1">
  Some people refer to it as a "front-end framework," while others are more specific and refer to it as a "CSS framework" or a "CSS library."
 </p>
 <p variant="body1">
  So, what is Bootstrap?
 </p>
 <p variant="body1">
  Simply put, Bootstrap is a library of CSS and JavaScript code that you can combine to quickly build visually appealing websites.
 </p>
 <p variant="body1">
  Modern web development is all about
  <strong>
   components
  </strong>
  —small pieces of reusable code that allow you to build websites quickly. Bootstrap comes with multiple components that enable the fast construction of different parts of a website.
 </p>
 <p variant="body1">
  Another important aspect of modern development is
  <strong>
   responsive grids
  </strong>
  , which allow web pages to adapt their layout and content depending on the device on which they are viewed. Bootstrap comes with a pre-made set of CSS rules for building a responsive grid.
 </p>
 <p variant="body1">
  Bootstrap is very popular among developers because it saves development time and provides a way to quickly build visually appealing prototypes and websites.
 </p>
 <p variant="body1">
  Bootstrap saves significant time because all the CSS code that styles its grid and pre-built components is already written. Instead of needing a high level of expertise in various CSS concepts, you can simply use the existing Bootstrap CSS classes to create visually appealing websites. This is indispensable when you need to quickly iterate on website layouts.
 </p>
 <p variant="body1">
  Once you know how Bootstrap works, you’ll have enough knowledge to tweak its styling, and a whole new world of development opens up to you.
 </p>
 <p variant="body1">
  Since Bootstrap is so popular, understanding how to work with it is a prerequisite in many web development companies. Additionally, you can be confident that you and your team members have a common design system, so you don't have to spend time deciding how to build one. You are free to jump from team to team, from project to project, and even from one company to another without needing to re-learn "their way of doing things."
 </p>
 <p variant="body1">
  All of these points make investing time in learning Bootstrap a great way to boost your web development skills. In this lesson, you’ll be introduced to the core concepts of Bootstrap and learn how to build web pages using it.
 </p>
</co-content>
